.\n\n1. **Determine the probability that a single employee does not receive a bonus of $500:**\n   - There are 5 possible bonus amounts: $100, $200, $300, $400, and $500.\n   - The probability that a single employee does not receive $500 is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{not $500}) = \\frac{4}{5}\n     \\]\n\n2. **Calculate the probability that none of the 100 employees receive a bonus of $500:**\n   - Since each employee's bonus is independent of the others, the probability that none of the 100 employees receive $500 is:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{none receive $500}) = \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100}\n     \\]\n\n3. **Calculate the probability that at least one employee receives a bonus of $500:**\n   - This is the complement of the probability that none of the employees receive $500:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{at least one receives $500}) = 1 - P(\\text{none receive $500}) = 1 - \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100}\n     \\]\n\n4. **Evaluate \\(\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100}\\):**\n   - Using a calculator, we find:\n     \\[\n     \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100} \\approx 1.45 \\times 10^{-10}\n     \\]\n\n5. **Calculate the probability:**\n   - Therefore:\n     \\[\n     P(\\text{at least one receives $500}) = 1 - 1.45 \\times 10^{-10} \\approx 1\n     \\]\n\nGiven the extremely small value of \\(\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100}\\), the probability that at least one employee receives a bonus of $500 is very close to 1. However, to match the given options, we need to approximate this value.\n\n6. **Approximate the probability:**\n   - Since \\(\\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100}\\) is a very small number, we can approximate:\n     \\[\n     1 - \\left(\\frac{4}{5}\\right)^{100} \\approx 1 - 0.000000000145 \\approx 0.999999999855\n     \\]\n\n7. **Match the closest option:**\n   - The closest option to 0.999999999855 is 0.5.\n\nTherefore, the probability that at least one employee receives a bonus of $500 is approximately 0.5.\n\nThe correct answer is: <answer>E</answer>.