      "text": " C. Some students with household registration in this city are third-grade students. <|User|>Now, please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n# Context:\nThere are 100 students in a class. 40 students are enrolled in a math course, 30 students are enrolled in a science course, and 10 students are enrolled in both courses. \n\n# Question:\nHow many students are enrolled in at least one of the two courses?\n# Options:\nA) 60\nB) 70\nC) 80\nD) 90\n<|Assistant|><think>\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to determine the number of students enrolled in at least one of the two courses (math or science). We can use the principle of inclusion and exclusion to find this number.\n\nLet's define:\n- \\( M \\) as the set of students enrolled in the math course.\n- \\( S \\) as the set of students enrolled in the science course.\n- \\( |M| \\) as the number of students enrolled in the math course.\n- \\( |S| \\) as the number of students enrolled in the science course.\n- \\( |M \\cap S| \\) as the number of students enrolled in both courses.\n- \\( |M \\cup S| \\) as the number of students enrolled in at least one of the two courses.\n\nFrom the context, we have:\n- \\( |M| = 40 \\)\n- \\( |S| = 30 \\)\n- \\( |M \\cap S| = 10 \\)\n\nThe principle of inclusion and exclusion for two sets states:\n\\[ |M \\cup S| = |M| + |S| - |M \\cap S| \\]\n\nSubstituting the given values into the formula:\n\\[ |M \\cup S| = 40 + 30 - 10 \\]\n\\[ |M \\cup S| = 60 \\]\n\nTherefore, the number of students enrolled in at least one of the two courses is 60.\n\n<answer>\nA\n</answer>\n</think> Therefore, the correct answer is A) 60. <|User|>Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n# Context:\nThere are 100 students in a class. 40 students are enrolled in a