messages:  [["Please solve the logical reasoning problem.\nGiven a context and a question, answer with the correct option: A, B, C or D. Please reason step by step.\n####\n# Context:\nEditorial: Our society has a vested interest in maintaining a political system in which candidates are free to adhere to their principles. Yet campaigning for elected office is extremely costly, and because only the wealthiest individuals are able to finance their own political campaigns, most candidates must seek funding from private sources. In so doing, the candidates are almost invariably obliged to compromise their principles. Thus, government itself should assume the cost of candidates' campaigns.\n\n# Question:\nBased on the above information, which one of the following principles, if valid, most helps to justify the conclusion as it is drawn in the argument?\n# Options:\nA) Candidates should not run for elected office if doing so would compel the candidates to compromise their principles.\nB) Voters should not support a candidate if that candidate is known to have accepted funding from private sources.\nC) Candidates wealthy enough to finance their own political campaigns should not be permitted to raise additional funds from private sources.\nD) The government should finance a given activity if doing so will further a vested interest of society.", '<think>.\nTo solve this logical reasoning problem, we need to identify the principle that most justifies the conclusion drawn in the editorial. The conclusion is that "government itself should assume the cost of candidates\' campaigns." Let\'s analyze each option to see which one best supports this conclusion.\n\nA) Candidates should not run for elected office if doing so would compel the candidates to compromise their principles.\n- This option suggests that candidates should not run for office if it forces them to compromise their principles. However, the editorial does not argue that candidates should not run for office; rather, it argues that the current system is flawed and needs to be changed. This option does not directly support the conclusion that the government should finance campaigns.\n\nB) Voters should not support a candidate if that candidate is known to have accepted funding from private sources.\n- This option is about voter behavior and does not address the justification for government financing of campaigns. It does not support the conclusion that the government should finance campaigns.\n\nC) Candidates wealthy enough to finance their own political campaigns should not be permitted to raise additional funds from private sources.\n- This option suggests a restriction on candidates who can finance their campaigns themselves. However, the editorial does not propose such a restriction. Instead, it argues for government financing of campaigns. This option does not support the conclusion.\n\nD) The government should finance a given activity if doing so will further a vested interest of society.\n- This option directly supports the conclusion. Therefore, my final option is: ']]
